{"record": "turn", "turn": 4, "seconds": 3.988, "response_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "stop_reason": "tool_use", "stop_details": null, "blocks": ["thinking", "tool_use"], "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 2139}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 2139, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5411, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 452, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 65}, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}, "tools": [{"id": "toolu_01XnYh7uUAJUqJQ7Z7CAXZqC", "name": "submit_answer", "arguments": {"answer": "In Kim, Lee, Park and Shin (arXiv:1601.05089), the best-fit point for ψψ̄ → h2h2 (about 100%, via ψψ̄ → h2 → h2h2) has m_ψ = 69.2 GeV and m_h2 = 35.7 GeV, close to m_ψ/2. They give ⟨σv⟩ = 2.26×10⁻²⁶ cm³ s⁻¹, with Ωh² = 0.121. The fit has χ² = 23.19 (p-value 0.39) and uses a \"moderate\" J-factor rescaling of 𝒥 = 2.2. I read this as the canonical J-factor being multiplied by 2.2. The section I read does not define 𝒥 explicitly, so that reading is an inference.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "1601.05089v3", "section_id": "3.4"}]}, "status": "answer", "error_kind": null, "seconds": 0.0}]}
